WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.537
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:18.816
Unit thirty-six: Misinformation and Society. In this unit, you will analyse how false information spreads and how to counter it.

4
00:00:19.817 --> 00:00:21.607
disinformation campaign.

5
00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:28.045
disinformation campaign means an organised effort to spread false information deliberately.

6
00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:31.471
The disinformation campaign targeted voters online.

7
00:00:32.171 --> 00:00:36.367
Your turn. Say: disinformation campaign.

8
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.765
disinformation campaign.

9
00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.343
conspiracy theory.

10
00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:49.804
conspiracy theory means a belief that a secret group is behind events, usually without evidence.

11
00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:53.393
Conspiracy theories spread quickly during crises.

12
00:00:54.093 --> 00:00:57.384
Your turn. Say: conspiracy theory.

13
00:01:00.084 --> 00:01:01.630
conspiracy theory.

14
00:01:02.617 --> 00:01:04.221
information disorder.

15
00:01:04.921 --> 00:01:09.556
information disorder means the polluted state of the information environment.

16
00:01:09.856 --> 00:01:12.982
Information disorder makes it hard to know what is true.

17
00:01:13.682 --> 00:01:17.472
Your turn. Say: information disorder.

18
00:01:20.172 --> 00:01:21.719
information disorder.

19
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:24.321
media manipulation.

20
00:01:25.021 --> 00:01:31.828
media manipulation means using media to mislead or influence people unfairly.

21
00:01:32.128 --> 00:01:35.892
Edited clips are a common form of media manipulation.

22
00:01:36.592 --> 00:01:40.394
Your turn. Say: media manipulation.

23
00:01:43.094 --> 00:01:44.896
media manipulation.

24
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:46.884
bot network.

25
00:01:47.584 --> 00:01:51.709
bot network means many fake automated accounts working together.

26
00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:54.600
A bot network made the hashtag trend.

27
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.324
Your turn. Say: bot network.

28
00:02:01.024 --> 00:02:02.106
bot network.

29
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:04.594
fact-based debate.

30
00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:09.604
fact-based debate means discussion grounded in verified evidence.

31
00:02:09.904 --> 00:02:12.623
Democracy needs fact-based debate.

32
00:02:13.323 --> 00:02:16.858
Your turn. Say: fact-based debate.

33
00:02:19.558 --> 00:02:21.139
fact-based debate.

34
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:23.546
epistemic trust.

35
00:02:24.246 --> 00:02:28.289
epistemic trust means trust in the sources from which we gain knowledge.

36
00:02:28.589 --> 00:02:31.273
Epistemic trust in experts is declining.

37
00:02:31.973 --> 00:02:35.322
Your turn. Say: epistemic trust.

38
00:02:38.022 --> 00:02:39.534
epistemic trust.

39
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.155
content moderation.

40
00:02:42.855 --> 00:02:47.188
content moderation means reviewing and removing harmful online content.

41
00:02:47.488 --> 00:02:51.032
Content moderation is difficult at a global scale.

42
00:02:51.732 --> 00:02:54.988
Your turn. Say: content moderation.

43
00:02:57.688 --> 00:02:58.979
content moderation.

44
00:02:59.983 --> 00:03:02.227
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:02.527 --> 00:03:06.303
My uncle shared a conspiracy theory about vaccines yesterday.

46
00:03:07.617 --> 00:03:10.893
If he had checked the source, he might not have shared it.

47
00:03:12.183 --> 00:03:15.367
He says he doesn't trust experts anymore.

48
00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:20.773
That loss of epistemic trust is at the heart of information disorder.

49
00:03:22.083 --> 00:03:25.209
Some of the posts seemed to come from a bot network.

50
00:03:26.517 --> 00:03:31.665
Very likely. Disinformation campaigns often use fake accounts to look popular.

51
00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.588
Shouldn't platforms do more content moderation?

52
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:42.461
They should. If moderation were stronger, those campaigns wouldn't have spread so fast.

53
00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.445
Grammar focus: Mixed conditionals in analysis.

54
00:03:47.745 --> 00:03:50.986
Mixed conditionals help analyse causes across time.

55
00:03:51.286 --> 00:03:54.726
If he had checked the source, he wouldn't believe it now.

56
00:03:55.026 --> 00:03:58.860
If moderation were stronger, the campaign wouldn't have spread.

57
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.201
Mixed conditionals are useful for analysing causes and consequences across time.

58
00:04:05.217 --> 00:04:07.881
Now read and listen. The Infodemic.

59
00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:13.477
During major crises, false information can spread faster than the facts.

60
00:04:14.783 --> 00:04:20.312
The World Health Organization even used the word 'infodemic' to describe this problem.

61
00:04:21.617 --> 00:04:27.715
Disinformation campaigns are deliberately designed to deceive, often using bot networks.

62
00:04:29.017 --> 00:04:34.998
Conspiracy theories, meanwhile, offer simple explanations for complex and frightening events.

63
00:04:36.283 --> 00:04:41.441
If people had greater epistemic trust in experts, such theories would struggle to spread.

64
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:48.395
Platforms have increased content moderation, but critics argue it remains inconsistent.

65
00:04:49.683 --> 00:04:54.771
Fact-based debate requires both reliable sources and critical audiences.

66
00:04:56.083 --> 00:05:01.659
Media literacy education may be the most powerful long-term solution.

67
00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.753
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:06.053 --> 00:05:09.713
Many fake automated accounts working together are a blank.

69
00:05:10.013 --> 00:05:15.791
A, bot network. B, conspiracy theory. C, content moderation.

70
00:05:19.783 --> 00:05:22.805
The answer is A: bot network.

71
00:05:24.317 --> 00:05:27.164
If she had left earlier, she blank here now.

72
00:05:27.464 --> 00:05:31.106
A, would be. B, would have been. C, is.

73
00:05:35.117 --> 00:05:37.499
The answer is A: would be.

74
00:05:38.983 --> 00:05:42.608
If I were braver, I blank the job last year.

75
00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:46.248
A, would take. B, would have taken. C, took.

76
00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.818
The answer is B: would have taken.

77
00:05:54.317 --> 00:05:56.827
Trust in the sources of our knowledge is blank.

78
00:05:57.127 --> 00:06:04.031
A, epistemic trust. B, information disorder. C, media manipulation.

79
00:06:08.017 --> 00:06:10.527
The answer is A: epistemic trust.

80
00:06:12.017 --> 00:06:16.756
According to the text, what may be the most powerful long-term solution?

81
00:06:17.056 --> 00:06:22.556
A, more bots. B, media literacy education. C, banning the internet.

82
00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:30.245
The answer is B: media literacy education.

83
00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:37.131
Now it's your turn. Part one: Who is responsible for stopping misinformation?

84
00:06:42.431 --> 00:06:49.341
Part two: Use a mixed conditional, assign responsibility to two groups, and justify it.

85
00:06:55.641 --> 00:07:09.268
Responsibility is shared. Platforms must moderate content, and schools must teach media literacy. If my generation had learned these skills earlier, fewer of us would be sharing false stories today.

86
00:07:09.568 --> 00:07:11.846
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:18.509
Great work today! You learned eight new words and mixed conditionals in analysis.

88
00:07:18.809 --> 00:07:22.681
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:22.981 --> 00:07:26.922
See you in Unit thirty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:27.222 --> 00:07:30.373
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

